WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:01.645
Module 1.

00:01.995 --> 00:04.410
How generative AI actually works.

00:04.760 --> 00:10.158
This module explains, without jargon, what a large language model does when it answers

00:10.158 --> 00:15.557
a legal question, and why that design produces both its strengths and its failures.

00:17.307 --> 00:22.990
By the end of this module you will be able to: explain in

00:22.990 --> 00:28.673
plain words how a large language model produces an answer; distinguish general chatbots

00:28.673 --> 00:34.356
from grounded legal research tools; recognise the built-in limits: training cut-off, inconsistency, sycophancy

00:34.356 --> 00:38.727
and context limits; explain why agentic AI raises the stakes.

00:40.477 --> 00:44.831
Welcome to the first module of the Rudra-X AI Practice Assurance programme.

00:45.181 --> 00:48.711
If you remember only one idea from this course, make it this one.

00:49.060 --> 00:52.230
A large language model predicts plausible text.

00:52.580 --> 00:54.136
It does not check facts.

00:54.486 --> 00:58.207
Everything else we cover, from hallucinated case law to

00:58.207 --> 01:01.928
the verification protocol, follows from that single design choice.

01:02.278 --> 01:05.138
So let's look at how these systems actually

01:05.138 --> 01:07.641
work, in plain language, with no mathematics.

01:09.391 --> 01:12.793
Here is what happens when you type a question and press enter.

01:13.143 --> 01:16.462
First, your prompt is broken into tokens, which are

01:16.462 --> 01:19.413
words and fragments of words converted into numbers.

01:19.763 --> 01:23.722
Second, the model scores every possible next token, based on patterns

01:23.722 --> 01:27.321
it learned from an enormous body of text during training.

01:27.671 --> 01:32.017
Third, it picks a likely token, adds it to the answer, and repeats

01:32.017 --> 01:36.030
the process, one fragment at a time, until the answer is complete.

01:36.380 --> 01:39.422
Finally, you read fluent, confident prose.

01:39.772 --> 01:41.977
Notice what is missing from these steps.

01:42.327 --> 01:45.788
At no point does a general model open a law report,

01:45.788 --> 01:48.934
query a court database or check whether a case exists.

01:49.283 --> 01:53.846
The fluency you see is produced by pattern matching, not by verification.

01:55.596 --> 01:57.674
This is why hallucinations happen.

01:58.024 --> 02:01.565
During training the model learned what legal text looks like.

02:01.915 --> 02:05.242
It learned that a citation has two party names, a

02:05.242 --> 02:08.568
volume, a reporter, a page, a court and a year.

02:08.918 --> 02:10.636
It learned how judges write.

02:10.986 --> 02:14.074
It learned what holdings on a topic usually say.

02:14.424 --> 02:18.452
So when you ask for authority, it can produce a citation in

02:18.452 --> 02:22.480
perfect format for a case that never existed, a quotation that sounds

02:22.480 --> 02:26.508
exactly like a judge but appears in no opinion, and a holding

02:26.508 --> 02:29.529
that fits your argument, because your question invited one.

02:29.879 --> 02:31.446
The model is not lying.

02:31.796 --> 02:33.631
It has no concept of truth.

02:33.981 --> 02:35.478
It is completing a pattern.

02:35.828 --> 02:38.632
That is exactly why the output is so

02:38.632 --> 02:41.436
convincing, and so dangerous when nobody checks it.

02:43.186 --> 02:47.307
Beyond hallucination, every user should know six built-in limits.

02:47.657 --> 02:49.480
First, the training cut-off.

02:49.830 --> 02:53.807
The model's knowledge stops at a date, so later cases, amended

02:53.807 --> 02:57.783
statutes and new court rules are invisible unless you supply them.

02:58.133 --> 03:00.118
Second, inconsistency.

03:00.468 --> 03:03.719
The same prompt can produce a different answer tomorrow.

03:04.069 --> 03:05.660
Third, sycophancy.

03:06.010 --> 03:08.599
Models tend to agree with the framing of your

03:08.599 --> 03:11.188
question, so a leading question gets a leading answer.

03:11.538 --> 03:13.604
Fourth, context limits.

03:13.954 --> 03:16.398
When you paste in a long document, parts

03:16.398 --> 03:18.842
of it may be ignored or summarised unevenly.

03:19.192 --> 03:21.363
Fifth, arithmetic and dates.

03:21.713 --> 03:24.226
Counting days to a filing deadline is exactly

03:24.226 --> 03:26.740
the kind of task these models get wrong.

03:27.090 --> 03:29.551
And sixth, jurisdiction blur.

03:29.901 --> 03:33.214
Rules from different states, and from other countries, get

03:33.214 --> 03:36.159
blended together into a confident but wrong answer.

03:37.909 --> 03:40.777
Not all AI tools behave the same way.

03:41.127 --> 03:46.081
A general consumer chatbot answers purely from its training patterns, and the provider's terms

03:46.081 --> 03:50.682
may allow your inputs to be retained or used to train future models.

03:51.032 --> 03:55.275
An enterprise-licensed version of the same chatbot usually has much better

03:55.275 --> 03:59.519
data terms, but it still hallucinates, so it still needs verification.

03:59.869 --> 04:05.627
Grounded legal research tools use a technique called retrieval augmented generation, or RAG.

04:05.977 --> 04:09.820
They first retrieve real documents and then write an answer based on them.

04:10.170 --> 04:13.003
That reduces error, but does not remove it.

04:13.353 --> 04:16.418
They can still misread a source, cite the wrong page,

04:16.418 --> 04:19.483
or rely on a case that has since been overturned.

04:19.833 --> 04:23.409
Then there are AI features embedded inside software you already

04:23.409 --> 04:26.985
use, where staff may not even realise AI is involved.

04:27.335 --> 04:32.501
And finally agentic AI, which plans and takes actions across several steps.

04:32.851 --> 04:35.695
That last category deserves its own slide.

04:37.445 --> 04:39.756
Agentic AI is the next stage.

04:40.106 --> 04:45.254
Instead of writing a draft for you to review, an agent can carry out

04:45.254 --> 04:50.403
actions: sending an email, filing a document, editing a contract in a shared folder,

04:50.403 --> 04:54.816
or deleting records, across several steps, without a person approving each one.

04:55.166 --> 04:56.594
Why does that matter?

04:56.944 --> 05:00.287
Because an error is no longer a draft sitting on your screen.

05:00.637 --> 05:04.701
It may already have reached a client, a court or the other side.

05:05.051 --> 05:08.418
The safeguard is simple to state and essential to enforce.

05:08.768 --> 05:12.850
No agentic tool should file, serve, send, sign, disclose or delete

05:12.850 --> 05:16.933
anything without prior approval from a reviewing lawyer, and a named

05:16.933 --> 05:21.016
person must be able to halt or reverse what it does.

05:22.766 --> 05:24.426
Let's recap the key points.

05:24.776 --> 05:28.364
AI predicts plausible text; it does not verify it.

05:28.714 --> 05:33.149
Hallucinations are a design feature of how the model works, not a rare bug.

05:33.499 --> 05:37.342
Grounded legal tools reduce errors but never eliminate them.

05:37.692 --> 05:41.918
Agentic AI must never act on the outside world without human approval.

05:43.668 --> 05:44.805
Knowledge check.

05:45.155 --> 05:49.056
A general chatbot gives you a case citation in perfect format.

05:49.406 --> 05:51.554
What does the perfect format tell you?

05:51.904 --> 05:55.886
Option A: The case exists, because the format is correct.

05:56.236 --> 05:59.626
Option B: Nothing about whether the case exists.

05:59.976 --> 06:04.260
Option C: The case exists but may be from another jurisdiction.

06:04.610 --> 06:07.327
Take a moment to decide before the answer appears.

06:13.077 --> 06:14.691
The answer is option B.

06:15.041 --> 06:17.851
Format is exactly what the model learned to imitate.

06:18.201 --> 06:21.266
Only a primary-source check tells you whether the

06:21.266 --> 06:23.947
case exists and says what is claimed.
